---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import RoundLegend from "../../components/RoundLegend.astro";
import data from "../../data/draft-and-winning.json";
import { summarize } from "../../lib/stats.js";

const s = summarize(data);
const [first, last] = s.seasons;
const pct = (x: number) => `${Math.round(x * 100)}%`;
const r2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const slope = data.meta.pickValue.slope;
const value = (pick: number) => Math.round(100 + slope * Math.log(pick));
const latest = data.seasons[data.seasons.length - 1];
const champ = latest.teams.find((t) => t.playoff === 5)!;
const champFirst = champ.summary.byRound["1"];
const champUdfa = champ.summary.byRound.udfa;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base
  title="Does the Draft Build Winners?"
  description={`How ${s.starters.toLocaleString()} NFL starters were acquired, and what it says about winning.`}
>
  <article>
    <header class="measure">
      <p class="kicker">The Draft</p>
      <h1>The Draft Builds Winners, Just Not the Way You’d Think</h1>
      <p class="dek">
        Across {last - first + 1} NFL seasons and {s.starters.toLocaleString()} starters, how high a team’s players were
        picked says almost nothing about its record. Whether the team picked them itself says more.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">Data through the {last}–{String(last + 1).slice(2)} season, including the Super Bowl · Updated {data.meta.generated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>
        Every spring, the draft is sold as the foundation of a franchise: get the first round right and the wins will
        follow. The {champ.name} won the most recent Super Bowl with {champFirst} first-round picks among their 22
        starters, which sounds like proof. They also started {champUdfa} players nobody drafted at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Below are all 32 teams’ most-used starters, ordered by regular-season record. Each dot is a player, shaded by the
        round he was drafted in.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" id="lead">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>Where every starter was drafted</h3>
        <p>The 11 offensive and 11 defensive players with the most snaps on each team. Hover a dot for the player.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Season" data-seasons>
        {data.seasons.map((x) => (
          <button type="button" data-season={x.season} aria-pressed={x.season === last ? "true" : "false"}>
            {x.season}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>
      <RoundLegend />
      <div class="chart" data-chart="lead"></div>
      <figcaption>Teams with bold names made the playoffs. Shaded row: Super Bowl champion. Source: nflverse.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>Draft pedigree barely moves the needle</h2>
      <p>
        To compare rosters, we scored each starter by the typical career value of the pick spent on him. The first
        overall pick is worth 100, the 16th about {value(16)}, the 65th about {value(65)} and a seventh-rounder about
        {value(240)}. Undrafted players are worth zero. Averaged across a team’s 22 starters, that “draft capital” has
        almost no relationship with winning.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="measure">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>Draft capital vs. regular-season record</h3>
        <p>Each dot is one team-season, {first}–{last}.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="legend">
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s1)"></span>Made playoffs</li>
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--udfa); opacity: 0.6"></span>Missed playoffs</li>
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s1); box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--ink)"></span>Won Super Bowl</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="chart" data-chart="capital"></div>
      <figcaption>
        Draft capital is the average pick value of a team’s 22 starters. A correlation (r) of 0 means no relationship; 1
        means a perfect one.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>
        The correlation is {r2(s.r.capital)}. A simpler measure, the share of starters who were first-round picks, does no
        better ({r2(s.r.firstRound)}). Expensive picks bust, cheap ones hit, and players move. A roster full of
        first-rounders tells you how much a team, or several teams, once spent. It doesn’t tell you how well.
      </p>

      <h2>What matters more is who did the drafting</h2>
      <p>
        The stronger signal is whether a starter was drafted by the team he now plays for. Across the decade, about
        {pct(s.homegrown.mean)} of starters were homegrown. Teams in the top quarter, at least {pct(s.homegrown.q3)}
        homegrown, made the playoffs {pct(s.homegrown.topRate)} of the time. Teams in the bottom quarter, {pct(s.homegrown.q1)}
        or fewer, made it {pct(s.homegrown.bottomRate)} of the time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="measure">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>Homegrown starters vs. regular-season record</h3>
        <p>Share of each team’s 22 starters it drafted itself.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="legend">
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s1)"></span>Made playoffs</li>
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--udfa); opacity: 0.6"></span>Missed playoffs</li>
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s1); box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--ink)"></span>Won Super Bowl</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="chart" data-chart="homegrown"></div>
      <figcaption>Relocated franchises count as the same team, so a player the Oakland Raiders drafted is homegrown in Las Vegas.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>
        At r = {r2(s.r.homegrown)}, it is still a modest relationship. Plenty of homegrown rosters lose, and some
        champions were assembled in free agency. But it points to something the round can’t capture: a team that keeps
        its own picks is usually a team that picked well, and has kept the scouts, coaches and system that made the picks
        work.
      </p>

      <h2>Champions lean on the second round</h2>
      <p>
        Sorting teams by how far they went in the playoffs shows the same pattern from another angle. The last
        {s.champs.length} Super Bowl winners started fewer first-round picks than the teams that missed the playoffs,
        {pct(s.shares.champs.r1)} to {pct(s.shares.missed.r1)}, and many more second-rounders,
        {pct(s.shares.champs.r2)} to {pct(s.shares.missed.r2)}.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="measure">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>How starters were acquired, by playoff result</h3>
        <p>Share of all starters on teams with each result, {first}–{last}.</p>
      </div>
      <RoundLegend />
      <div class="chart" data-chart="mix"></div>
      <figcaption>Only one team a season wins or loses the Super Bowl, so those rows rest on {s.champs.length} teams each and can swing with a few players.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The exception is quarterback</h2>
      <p>
        At most positions, playoff teams and also-rans start first-round picks at about the same rate. Quarterback is
        the clear exception. {pct(s.qb.made)} of playoff teams’ starting quarterbacks were first-round picks, compared
        with {pct(s.qb.missed)} for teams that missed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="measure">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>Share of starters who were first-round picks, by position</h3>
        <p>{first}–{last}. Positions as listed by Pro Football Reference.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="legend">
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s1)"></span>Playoff teams</li>
        <li><span class="swatch" style="background: var(--s2)"></span>Teams that missed</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="chart" data-chart="positions"></div>
      <figcaption>Edge rushers are listed as defensive linemen or linebackers depending on the team’s scheme, so they are not separated here.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The undrafted starter is disappearing</h2>
      <p>
        One more trend runs underneath all of this. In {first}, {pct(s.bySeason[0].udfa)} of starters had gone undrafted.
        By {last}, it was {pct(s.bySeason[s.bySeason.length - 1].udfa)}. Teams are filling more of their lineups with
        drafted players, which makes it more important that the picks are good, not just high.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="measure">
      <div class="chart-head">
        <h3>Share of starters who went undrafted</h3>
        <p>All 32 teams, by season.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart" data-chart="udfa"></div>
    </figure>

    <section class="measure notes">
      <h2>Explore a team</h2>
      <p class="team-links">
        {latest.teams
          .slice()
          .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))
          .map((t, i, arr) => (
            <>
              <a href={`${base}draft/teams/${t.franchise.toLowerCase()}/`}>{t.name}</a>
              {i < arr.length - 1 ? " · " : ""}
            </>
          ))}
      </p>

      <h2>How we did this</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Starters.</strong> For every team and season, the {data.meta.starterRule.toLowerCase()}. Snap counts come
        from Pro Football Reference. A player traded midseason counts only for the team where he played those snaps.
        Kickers, punters and special teams are excluded.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Draft position.</strong> Each starter is matched to the draft pick that brought him into the league,
        regardless of which team made it. Players with no draft record are counted as undrafted. A handful of
        supplemental-draft picks are counted in the round the pick was spent.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick value.</strong> We fit a curve to the average career Approximate Value (a Pro Football Reference
        measure of player contribution) produced by every draft slot from {data.meta.pickValue.fitDrafts[0]} to
        {data.meta.pickValue.fitDrafts[1]}, then rescaled it so the first pick equals 100.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Records.</strong> Regular-season win percentage, with ties counted as half a win. Playoff results run
        from missing the playoffs to winning the Super Bowl.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Data.</strong> All inputs are free, public files from the
        <a href="https://github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data">nflverse</a> project. Correlations here describe
        association, not cause.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  import data from "../../data/draft-and-winning.json";
  import { leadStrip } from "../../charts/leadStrip.js";
  import { scatter, tierDot } from "../../charts/scatter.js";
  import { roundMixBars, dumbbell, seasonLine } from "../../charts/compare.js";
  import { allTeams, roundShares, positionFirstRound } from "../../lib/stats.js";
  import { NICKNAMES, PLAYOFF_LONG, record } from "../../lib/draft.js";

  const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
  const teams = allTeams(data);
  const latest = data.seasons[data.seasons.length - 1];
  const champ = latest.teams.find((t) => t.playoff === 5);
  const el = (name) => document.querySelector(`[data-chart="${name}"]`);
  let season = latest;

  const pctFormat = (v) => `${Math.round(v * 100)}%`;
  const tier = (p) => (p === 5 ? 2 : p > 0 ? 1 : 0);
  const teamScatter = (name, metric, xAxis) => {
    const points = teams.map((t) => ({ ...t, x: t.summary[metric], y: t.winPct }));
    scatter(el(name), points, {
      x: xAxis,
      y: { label: "Regular-season win %", format: pctFormat, domain: [0, 1] },
      dot: (d) => tierDot(tier(d.playoff)),
      order: (d) => tier(d.playoff),
      tip: (d) =>
        `<strong>${d.season} ${NICKNAMES[d.franchise]}</strong><br>${record(d)} · ${PLAYOFF_LONG[d.playoff]}<br><span class="muted">${xAxis.label}: ${xAxis.format(d.x)}</span>`,
      label: (d) => (d.season === latest.season && d.team === champ.team ? `${d.season} ${NICKNAMES[d.franchise]}` : null),
    });
  };
  const draw = {
    lead: () => leadStrip(el("lead"), season, { teamHref: (f) => `${base}draft/teams/${f.toLowerCase()}/` }),
    capital: () => teamScatter("capital", "capital", { label: "Draft capital", format: (v) => v.toFixed(0) }),
    homegrown: () =>
      teamScatter("homegrown", "homegrownShare", { label: "Homegrown starters", format: pctFormat, domain: [0, 1] }),
    mix: () =>
      roundMixBars(
        el("mix"),
        [5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0].map((p) => {
          const g = teams.filter((t) => t.playoff === p);
          return { label: PLAYOFF_LONG[p], n: g.length, shares: roundShares(g), strong: p === 5 };
        }),
      ),
    positions: () => dumbbell(el("positions"), positionFirstRound(teams), { aLabel: "Playoff teams", bLabel: "Missed" }),
    udfa: () =>
      seasonLine(
        el("udfa"),
        data.seasons.map((s) => ({ season: s.season, v: roundShares(s.teams).udfa })),
        { yLabel: "Starters undrafted" },
      ),
  };

  const drawAll = () => Object.values(draw).forEach((f) => f());
  drawAll();

  let lastWidth = window.innerWidth;
  window.addEventListener("resize", () => {
    if (window.innerWidth === lastWidth) return;
    lastWidth = window.innerWidth;
    drawAll();
  });

  document.querySelectorAll("[data-seasons] button").forEach((btn) => {
    btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      season = data.seasons.find((s) => s.season === Number(btn.dataset.season));
      document.querySelectorAll("[data-seasons] button").forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
      draw.lead();
    });
  });
</script>
